WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.935
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.935 --> 00:00:02.739
Thursday the twenty-second.

00:00:02.739 --> 00:00:07.749
Easy weather, a full workday, and one tight handoff right at the end of it.

00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:09.686
While you move, try this one.

00:00:09.686 --> 00:00:12.826
What presidential nickname helped the word O.K.

00:00:12.826 --> 00:00:13.695
stick around?

00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:15.031
Answer's at the end.

00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:16.500
Weather's a non-issue.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:26.521
Sunny and dry all day, high in the upper sixties, with a north wind around ten to twenty miles an hour that mostly hits you from the side on the ride.

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:27.456
Okay, the day.

00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:32.733
You're out for a run at seven, the easy zone two kind, thirty to forty minutes.

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.474
Keep it conversational and soft-pedal the ride in after.

00:00:36.474 --> 00:00:38.612
Then Godinger from nine to five.

00:00:38.612 --> 00:00:45.626
The tricky bit is an appointment at five, which means there's no gap between leaving work and showing up.

00:00:45.626 --> 00:00:50.302
If you can, wrap up a few minutes before five and roll straight there.

00:00:50.302 --> 00:00:53.910
And don't forget the payment you're supposed to bring.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.981
After that, the evening's open.

00:00:55.981 --> 00:00:56.916
Alright, news.

00:00:56.916 --> 00:01:02.394
Adobe quietly started listing Lightroom on the iPhone App Store as Lightroom, A.I.

00:01:02.394 --> 00:01:03.262
Photo Editor.

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:11.145
It looks more like a search keyword than an official rename, but it landed alongside a new free tool called Open Eyes.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:19.027
When Lightroom spots someone blinking in a portrait or a group shot, it offers a one-tap fix that generates open eyes.

00:01:19.027 --> 00:01:24.839
You can even feed it other photos of that person so the eyes actually look like theirs.

00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:30.985
Some photographers online aren't thrilled with the name or the push toward generative fixes.

00:01:30.985 --> 00:01:34.793
Over in Ukraine, there's a small shift in the peace talk.

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:35.194
A U.S.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:42.742
envoy met with Putin, and during that visit Russia released a seventy-four-year-old American it had been holding.

00:01:42.742 --> 00:01:52.763
The Kremlin is now saying it's open to new talks, and Zelenskyy says he'd back freezing the front line where it is as a first step toward a ceasefire.

00:01:52.763 --> 00:01:58.174
Overnight, though, Russian strikes still killed at least seven people in Ukraine.

00:01:58.174 --> 00:02:01.647
So the words are softening faster than the fighting.

00:02:01.647 --> 00:02:03.451
Here's one for the weekend.

00:02:03.451 --> 00:02:09.864
There's a new movie theater on Troutman Street, in Bushwick, that shows nothing but short films.

00:02:09.864 --> 00:02:18.949
It's about forty seats, a hand-painted room, a little snack bar with popcorn, and a split-flap board that announces what's playing next.

00:02:18.949 --> 00:02:20.820
The fun part is how you pay.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.957
You don't buy a ticket up front.

00:02:22.957 --> 00:02:33.512
Each short has its own price based on length, from about a dollar for a one or two minute film up to nine for the long ones, and you settle up on the way out.

00:02:33.512 --> 00:02:38.923
It's open every night from five to ten, so Saturday evening is an easy ride over.

00:02:38.923 --> 00:02:44.000
A few hours of shorts can add up to thirty or forty bucks, so pace yourself.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.338
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:51.081
Tonight, brush your teeth standing on one leg, and switch legs halfway.

00:02:51.081 --> 00:02:55.757
If that's easy, close your eyes for the last ten seconds on each side.

00:02:55.757 --> 00:03:04.976
It sounds silly, but balance is one of the first things to slip when you only train in straight lines, and runners and cyclists mostly do.

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:09.853
Two minutes, zero equipment, and you'll feel which ankle is the lazy one.

00:03:09.853 --> 00:03:12.325
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:18.003
Remember setting up the memo's audio pipeline, and that target of minus sixteen LUFS?

00:03:18.003 --> 00:03:19.472
People just say luffs.

00:03:19.472 --> 00:03:22.545
Here's what that number is actually measuring.

00:03:22.545 --> 00:03:24.817
There are two ways to set a level.

00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:27.088
The old way is peak normalization.

00:03:27.088 --> 00:03:34.303
You find the single loudest moment in the file and turn everything up until that moment touches the ceiling.

00:03:34.303 --> 00:03:39.981
The problem is that one cough or one plosive decides the volume of the whole episode.

00:03:39.981 --> 00:03:43.989
Loudness normalization works on what your ear hears instead.

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:52.940
It runs the audio through a filter that roughly matches human hearing, so deep bass counts for less and the upper mids count for more.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:04:00.489
Then it averages that across the whole file, and it ignores the silent gaps so pauses don't drag the number down.

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:03.762
That average is the integrated loudness, in LUFS.

00:04:03.762 --> 00:04:06.902
One unit up or down is the same as one decibel.

00:04:06.902 --> 00:04:10.777
Minus sixteen is the common target for podcasts on phones.

00:04:10.777 --> 00:04:17.190
TV broadcast in Europe sits much quieter, at minus twenty-three, because living rooms are quiet.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.793
Earbuds outside are not.

00:04:18.793 --> 00:04:29.882
At minus sixteen, a voice stays clear over traffic without you cranking it, and every episode lands at the same level, so you never reach for the volume between days.

00:04:29.882 --> 00:04:41.573
The pipeline also keeps a true peak ceiling, around minus one, because turning audio into MP3 can push a peak a little higher than it was, and that's where clipping sneaks in.

00:04:41.573 --> 00:04:47.251
So: run at seven, Godinger till five, then straight to that five o'clock appointment.

00:04:47.251 --> 00:04:53.397
And the answer from the top: Old Kinderhook, Martin Van Buren's nickname, in eighteen-forty.

00:04:53.397 --> 00:04:55.000
Have a smooth run, Ziad.
